# `none` is the call's HEAD here rather than an argument. The exhaustiveness
# check reads a call's arguments, so a literal none handed to a group with no
# arm for it is refused at compile time now — the error corpus pins that in
# a_none_reaches_a_group_with_no_arm_for_it. Calling a none is a different
# mistake, the check says nothing about it, and it stays the runtime's to
# report. It used to be written `list/map [1 2] none`, which the check refuses.
nothing = none

pub play = print "{nothing 1}"
